## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects/functions compare by reference.

```jsx
// New object reference every render → child always re-renders
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Every render of the parent creates a *new* object/function literal, so the child sees a changed prop even though the data is identical.

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wrap a component to skip re-renders when props haven't changed (shallow comparison). Only useful if you also stabilize the props being passed.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, data }) => <button onClick={onClick}>{data.label}</button>);
```

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders.

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value or object reference.

```jsx
const style = useMemo(() => ({ color: theme.primary }), [theme.primary]);
const filtered = useMemo(() => items.filter(x => x.active), [items]);
```

**When to reach for each:**
| Tool | Use when |
|------|----------|
| `React.memo` | Child is expensive to render and receives stable props |
| `useCallback` | Passing a callback to a memoized child or as a `useEffect` dep |
| `useMemo` | Computing an expensive value, or stabilizing an object/array reference |

**Don't over-memoize.** These have their own cost (memory, comparison overhead). Skip them for cheap components or frequently-changing deps.

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context *value reference* changes — even if the data inside is the same.

```jsx
// Bad: new object every render
<MyContext.Provider value={{ user, logout }}>
```

```jsx
// Better: memoize the value
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, logout }), [user, logout]);
<MyContext.Provider value={value}>
```

Also split contexts by update frequency. A single `AppContext` holding both static config and frequently-changing auth state forces every consumer to re-render on auth changes.

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every parent render, even when `items` hasn't changed:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleSelect = (id) => console.log('selected', id); // new ref each render
  const config = { sortBy: 'name' };                         // new ref each render

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>+{count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} config={config} />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect, config }) {
  console.log('ExpensiveList rendered'); // fires on every parent render
  return items.map(item => <Item key={item.id} item={item} onSelect={onSelect} />);
}
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` actually changes:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => console.log('selected', id), []); // stable ref
  const config = useMemo(() => ({ sortBy: 'name' }), []);                     // stable ref

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>+{count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} config={config} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect, config }) {
  console.log('ExpensiveList rendered'); // only fires when items/onSelect/config change
  return items.map(item => <Item key={item.id} item={item} onSelect={onSelect} />);
});
```

**The mental model:** `React.memo` is the gate; `useCallback`/`useMemo` are what make the props stable enough for the gate to hold.
